## Workflows

1. Bootstrap: detect_stack → init_project → install_toolchain →
   security_scan_full (or scan_wordpress) → set_baseline
2. Before PR: review_pr → triage_findings → prioritize_findings → suggest_fix
3. Audit: audit_executive (stack-aware) → risk_score → compliance_evidence
4. Active DAST: map_attack_surface → scan_dast (app must already be running)
5. Reachability: map_attack_surface → validate_finding (needs a prior surface
   snapshot; run right after a SAST scan, not scan_dast — it validates
   whichever scan completed most recently)

## CI (headless)

`node cli/dev-guardian.mjs scan` — same pipeline, no MCP, gated on a committed
`.guardian/baseline.json` (`baseline update` writes it, `scan` never does). Exit
codes: 0 pass, 1 gate failed, 2 incomplete scan (never read as a pass), 3 usage
error. Install via `git clone --depth 1` + `npm ci` in mcp/, not npx — see the
README's "Run scans in CI" for a copy-pasteable job. `--start-command` (DAST pass)
is argv-only — refused outright if `.guardian/ci.json` declares it (pwn-request
guard: a fork's PR must never get code execution on the runner). Leaves
`.guardian/reports/` in the workspace — add `.guardian/` to .gitignore by hand; the
MCP server does this on its own startup, this CLI never does.

## Local dashboard (offline, read-only)

`node cli/dev-guardian.mjs status` — one-screen summary (risk, open
findings/CVEs, both deltas, top-3 hotspots by count, missing-scanner
consequences, suppressions); `dashboard` — same snapshot as a
self-contained `.guardian/dashboard.html`, opened automatically only on a
TTY (`--no-open` / `--out <path>`). Read-only — no scan, no mutation, no
socket — and always exits 0 once rendered (report, not gate; 3 is the only
other code, on a usage error). The page is a snapshot, not live — it won't
update on a later scan, regenerate it. Window is the latest scan + two
deltas only, no multi-week trend.

## Anti-patterns

- Don't `security_scan_full` for tiny changes — use review_pr
- Don't suppress_finding before triage_findings
- Don't shell out to scanners — lose persistence + baselines
- Don't run scan_wordpress on non-WP — detect_stack first
- Don't run scan_dast before map_attack_surface — it refuses with
  no_surface_snapshot
- Don't read a clean scan_dast result as "no injection vulnerabilities" —
  the own engine sends none; that's nuclei's job, opt-in and origin-scoped
- Don't treat validate_finding's unreachable as safety proof — import-graph
  signal, unavailable for Ruby/Java/C#/PHP, blind to dynamic imports
